# Why operations teams lose a day a week
A white paper is read, not presented: whole paragraphs, sections and sub-sections, the figures where they prove a point.

Operations teams rarely lack tools. They lack a single place where a request, the procedure that answers it and the person who carries it out meet. Each of these lives in its own tool, and every handover between two tools is a moment where context is lost and has to be rebuilt by hand.

This paper describes the cost of that fragmentation in the teams Northwind works with, the way the work flows once it is gathered in one workspace, and the steps a team follows to get there without stopping the service it runs.

## Where the time goes

The hours are not lost in the work itself. They are lost around it: finding the latest version of a procedure, asking who is on call this week, copying a request from a mail into a ticket, writing on Monday the report of what happened on Friday.[^survey]

[^survey]: Northwind customer survey, spring of the year of this paper: the figures below are its own.

::: metrics
- 6.5 h | a week per person | Spent looking for context rather than acting on it.
- 4 | tools on average | Between a request and its resolution.
- 38% | of requests | Reopened at least once for a missing detail. {.hero}
:::

### Three moments where context is lost

1. When a request arrives by mail and is copied into a ticket, its history stays behind in the mailbox.
2. When the person on call changes, what the previous one knew leaves with them.
3. When the manager writes the weekly report, the facts are gathered again from every tool.

> The work was never the problem. Finding where the work was described was.

## What a gathered workspace changes

When the request, the runbook and the rota sit in the same place, each handover keeps its context. The person who takes over reads what was done, the procedure that applies and who to call, without leaving the page.

The effect shows first on the requests that come back. A request answered with the right runbook at hand is answered once.[^reopen]

[^reopen]: Share of requests reopened at least once, month by month, across the teams of the survey.

::: chart {.bar}
unit: %
- January | 41
- February | 39
- March | 30
- April | 24
- May | 19
- June | 17
:::

